# `tests` in `paths:` is a DELIBERATE, repo-local exception to the fleet rule.
# Every other Jardis package analyses `src` only — Bescheid Rolf 2026-08-09:
# "ich dachte es reicht wenn src sauber geprüft ist, die tests habe ich bewusst
# ausgeklammert". That decision is right for test-code hygiene: a finding in a
# test reaches no consumer, and `composer.json`'s `autoload` covers only
# `JardisCore\App\ -> src/` (tests and examples are `autoload-dev`).
#
# This repo deviates for ONE reason, and the exception is meant to stay that
# narrow: it is the only package in the fleet where a class in the test tree
# IMPLEMENTS A PUBLISHED INTERFACE — `tests/Support/DomainResponse` against
# `JardisSupport\Contract\Kernel\DomainResponseInterface`. That is not hygiene;
# it is a second, competing statement about the API, and an unchecked one is
# free to contradict the Contract.
#
# It did. Until 2026-08-09 that fake declared `$data`/`$errors` as flat
# `array<string, mixed>` while the interface promises context-keyed maps, and
# seven test sites built a response shape the generated pipeline cannot produce
# (every generated `ContextResponse::getData()` returns `[$context => $data]` —
# 13 of 13 instances in the corpus, identical). Tests are the most-read form of
# documentation; a wrong shape there teaches the wrong shape.
#
# Cost of this exception, measured: 0 findings. Both `analyse /app/src` and the
# paths below report `[OK] No errors` at level 8. If that ever changes, the
# question to ask first is whether a test contradicts a Contract — not whether
# to drop `tests` from this list.
#
# Full evidence: claude/docs/standortbestimmung-php-2026-08/belege-s55/
# NACHPRUEFUNG-p45-hauptdialog.md
parameters:
    level: 8
    paths:
        - src
        - tests
